        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "fine_needle_aspiration_needle_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. High-yield OSCE questions and answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "fine_needle_aspiration_needle_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a fine-needle aspiration needle used to obtain cells or fluid from a lesion for cytological examination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the usual gauge?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Approximately 22–25G is common for many FNA procedures, although the appropriate size varies."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between aspiration and non-aspiration FNA?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Aspiration FNA uses negative pressure generated by a syringe, while non-aspiration FNA relies mainly on capillary action."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the most important limitation of FNA in suspected breast cancer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It does not usually preserve tissue architecture and may not establish whether a carcinoma is invasive."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What are the common complications?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain, bruising and minor bleeding; infection and vasovagal reactions are less common."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What should you do if the FNA result is non-diagnostic?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess the clinical and imaging findings and arrange repeat image-guided sampling or core biopsy as appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why is FNA useful for breast cysts?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can aspirate cyst fluid, potentially relieving symptoms while allowing further assessment when indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
